[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Java Language Overview and Platform Evolution",
        "content": "# Java Language Overview and Platform Evolution\n\nJava is a general-purpose, class-based programming language used to build backend services, enterprise systems, automation tools, financial applications and cloud-hosted software. Its execution model separates source code from the operating system's native machine instructions. Developers write Java source code, compile it into bytecode, and execute that bytecode using a compatible Java Virtual Machine (JVM).\n\nJava is statically and strongly typed. Variables have declared types, and the compiler checks many invalid operations before an application starts. Java also provides object-oriented programming, exception handling, multithreading, networking libraries, collections, security facilities and automatic memory management.\n\nJava has evolved across many releases. Lambda expressions and streams introduced functional-style data processing. The module system added stronger application modularity. Records simplified immutable data-carrier classes. Pattern matching and other language improvements have reduced boilerplate and improved readability. A project must use language features supported by its configured Java release.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\npublic class HelloJava {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, Java!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe class contains the program entry point. When launched, the JVM executes the `main` method and prints the message.\n\nJava's platform portability does not mean every Java application automatically runs everywhere. The target machine still needs a compatible runtime, required dependencies, supported operating-system features and correct configuration.\n\nIn DevOps work, Java knowledge helps teams select JDK versions, configure CI agents, compile and test applications, package JAR files, build container images and diagnose runtime failures. Record the Java version used by the build and runtime environments so that version differences do not create unexpected deployment problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "JDK, JRE and JVM — Roles and Differences",
        "content": "# JDK, JRE and JVM — Roles and Differences\n\nThe JVM, JRE and JDK are related parts of the Java platform, but they have different responsibilities.\n\nThe Java Virtual Machine executes Java bytecode. It loads classes, verifies bytecode, manages runtime memory, executes threads and performs garbage collection. JVM implementations are specific to operating systems and processor architectures, even though Java bytecode is designed to be portable.\n\nThe Java Runtime Environment traditionally describes the JVM together with the libraries and supporting files required to run Java applications. A machine that only executes an already-built application needs runtime capabilities, not necessarily compiler tools. Modern Java distributions do not always provide a separate JRE download; teams often use a JDK distribution or create a reduced runtime image.\n\nThe Java Development Kit includes runtime capabilities and development tools. Important tools include `javac` for compilation, `java` for launching programs, `jar` for archive operations, and diagnostic tools such as `jcmd` and `jstack`.\n\nCheck the installed tools:\n\n```bash\njava -version\njavac -version\n```\n\n`java -version` reports the runtime launcher version. `javac -version` reports the compiler version. If the first command works but the second does not, the machine may lack compiler tools or its JDK `bin` directory may not be configured in PATH.\n\nTypical responsibilities:\n\n- JVM: executes bytecode and manages runtime behavior.\n- JRE: provides the environment required to run Java applications.\n- JDK: provides development, compilation and diagnostic tools alongside runtime capabilities.\n\nA CI build agent normally needs a JDK because it compiles and tests source code. A runtime container may use a smaller runtime-focused image if compilation has already completed. Keep the build and runtime versions compatible, verify the actual image contents and use controlled image versions rather than depending on an unpinned `latest` tag."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Java Source Code, Compilation and Bytecode",
        "content": "# Java Source Code, Compilation and Bytecode\n\nJava source code is normally stored in `.java` files. The Java compiler translates valid source code into bytecode stored in `.class` files. Bytecode is an instruction format understood by a compatible JVM rather than native instructions for one particular operating system.\n\nCreate `Greeting.java`:\n\n```java\npublic class Greeting {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String message = \"Build completed\";\n        System.out.println(message);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCompile the source:\n\n```bash\njavac Greeting.java\n```\n\nA successful compilation creates `Greeting.class`. Launch it with:\n\n```bash\njava Greeting\n```\n\nThe launcher locates the class, starts the JVM and invokes its `main` method. Supply the class name without the `.class` extension.\n\nCompilation checks syntax, type compatibility, access rules and other language constraints. A compilation error prevents the affected class from being generated. Successful compilation does not guarantee correct business behavior: runtime exceptions, incorrect logic, missing configuration and unavailable external services can still cause failures.\n\nCompiled output can be placed in a separate directory:\n\n```bash\njavac -d out Greeting.java\njava -cp out Greeting\n```\n\nThe `-d` option selects the output directory. The `-cp` option specifies the classpath used to locate classes and dependencies.\n\nJava applications are often packaged into JAR archives. A JAR can contain compiled classes, resources and metadata describing the entry point. Build tools such as Maven and Gradle automate compilation, dependency resolution, testing and packaging.\n\nIn CI/CD, compile and test using a known JDK, package the tested output and publish the resulting artifact. Promote the same artifact through environments instead of rebuilding different binaries for development, staging and production. This makes releases easier to reproduce and investigate."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Primitive Data Types and Reference Types",
        "content": "# Primitive Data Types and Reference Types\n\nJava defines eight primitive data types: `byte`, `short`, `int`, `long`, `float`, `double`, `char` and `boolean`.\n\nInteger types represent whole numbers with different ranges. `float` and `double` represent floating-point values. `char` represents a UTF-16 code unit, and `boolean` represents either `true` or `false`.\n\n```java\nint retryCount = 3;\nlong requestId = 9876543210L;\ndouble responseTime = 0.245;\nchar grade = 'A';\nboolean deploymentSuccessful = true;\n```\n\nThe `L` suffix identifies a long literal. Decimal literals are treated as `double` by default. Use an `f` suffix when assigning a literal to a `float`.\n\nReference types include classes, arrays, interfaces, enums and records. A reference variable refers to an object rather than containing the complete object value. A reference can also contain `null`, meaning it currently refers to no object.\n\n```java\nString serviceName = \"payment-api\";\nString anotherName = serviceName;\n\nint[] ports = {8080, 8081};\n```\n\nAssigning `serviceName` to `anotherName` does not create a new String object. Both variables refer to the same object. This distinction is especially important with mutable objects because changes made through one reference may be visible through another.\n\nPrimitive variables cannot be assigned `null`. Reference variables can. Java provides wrapper classes such as `Integer`, `Long`, `Double` and `Boolean` when an object representation is needed. Collections store objects, so developers commonly use types such as `List<Integer>` instead of a primitive collection declaration.\n\nChoose data types according to the meaning and valid range of the data. For financial calculations requiring decimal precision, `BigDecimal` is often more appropriate than binary floating-point types. Understand reference sharing when debugging unexpected state changes and designing application models."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Variables, Scope and Type Conversion",
        "content": "# Variables, Scope and Type Conversion\n\nA variable is a named location associated with a data type. Java has local variables, instance variables and static variables.\n\nA local variable is declared inside a method, constructor or block and is accessible only within that scope. An instance variable belongs to an object, so separate objects have separate instance state. A static variable belongs to the class and is shared by its instances.\n\n```java\npublic class DeploymentConfig {\n    static int defaultPort = 8080;\n    String environment;\n\n    public void start() {\n        int retryLimit = 3;\n        System.out.println(retryLimit);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nLocal variables must be definitely assigned before they are read. Instance and static fields receive default values during initialization. A variable declared inside a block cannot be accessed after that block ends.\n\nJava supports widening conversions such as `int` to `long`:\n\n```java\nint attempts = 5;\nlong totalAttempts = attempts;\n```\n\nNarrowing conversions may lose information and require an explicit cast:\n\n```java\ndouble percentage = 98.75;\nint wholePercentage = (int) percentage;\n```\n\nThe result is `98` because the cast truncates the fractional part; it does not round the value.\n\nText can be converted into numbers using parsing methods:\n\n```java\nString portText = \"8080\";\nint port = Integer.parseInt(portText);\n```\n\nInvalid input causes a `NumberFormatException`. Validate data received from environment variables, files and API requests before using it.\n\nVariable names should explain their purpose. Prefer `connectionTimeoutSeconds` over unclear names such as `x`. In production applications, central configuration binding and validation reduce scattered conversions and make configuration errors easier to diagnose."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Operators and Expression Evaluation",
        "content": "# Operators and Expression Evaluation\n\nJava operators perform calculations, comparisons, logical checks and assignments. Arithmetic operators include `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `%`. Comparison operators include `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` and their inclusive forms. Logical operators include `&&`, `||` and `!`.\n\n```java\nint totalRequests = 120;\nint failedRequests = 6;\n\ndouble failureRate =\n    (double) failedRequests / totalRequests * 100;\n\nboolean alertRequired = failureRate > 5.0;\n\nSystem.out.println(failureRate);\nSystem.out.println(alertRequired);\n```\n\nThe cast to `double` prevents integer division from discarding the fractional part. Without the cast, dividing two integers would produce an integer result before multiplication.\n\nJava follows operator precedence rules. Multiplication and division are evaluated before addition and subtraction unless parentheses change the order. Use parentheses when they make a business calculation clearer.\n\nThe `&&` and `||` operators use short-circuit evaluation. With `&&`, the right side is evaluated only if the left side is true. With `||`, the right side is evaluated only if the left side is false.\n\n```java\nString environment = System.getenv(\"APP_ENV\");\n\nif (environment != null && environment.equals(\"production\")) {\n    System.out.println(\"Production configuration selected\");\n}\n```\n\nThe null check prevents the second condition from dereferencing a null value.\n\nFor objects such as strings, use `equals()` to compare content. The `==` operator compares reference identity for objects.\n\nWatch for integer overflow, integer division, floating-point precision and expressions with unclear precedence. Keep complicated calculations readable, use named intermediate variables and test boundary values such as zero, negative numbers and maximum expected counts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Conditional Statements and Control Flow",
        "content": "# Conditional Statements and Control Flow\n\nControl flow determines which statements execute. Java uses `if`, `else if` and `else` for conditional decisions. An `if` condition must evaluate to a boolean value.\n\n```java\nint httpStatus = 503;\n\nif (httpStatus >= 200 && httpStatus < 300) {\n    System.out.println(\"Request succeeded\");\n} else if (httpStatus >= 500) {\n    System.out.println(\"Server-side failure\");\n} else if (httpStatus >= 400) {\n    System.out.println(\"Client-side failure\");\n} else {\n    System.out.println(\"Other response\");\n}\n```\n\nThe order of conditions matters. A broad condition placed before a more specific condition may prevent the later branch from executing. Keep conditions understandable and separate complex business rules into helper methods.\n\nA switch statement is useful when one expression is compared against several alternatives. Modern Java releases support switch expressions:\n\n```java\nString severity = switch (httpStatus / 100) {\n    case 2 -> \"SUCCESS\";\n    case 4 -> \"CLIENT_ERROR\";\n    case 5 -> \"SERVER_ERROR\";\n    default -> \"OTHER\";\n};\n```\n\nUse syntax supported by the Java release configured in the project. Traditional switch statements can fall through when `break` is omitted. Arrow-style switch rules avoid that traditional fall-through behavior.\n\nControl flow is central to application decisions: choosing an environment, classifying HTTP responses, validating input or determining whether a deployment should continue. Test every meaningful branch, including unexpected and boundary values. A condition that is never tested can hide defects until production traffic reaches it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Loops, Branching and Iteration",
        "content": "# Loops, Branching and Iteration\n\nLoops repeat a block of code while a condition remains satisfied or while elements remain in a sequence. Java provides `for`, enhanced `for`, `while` and `do-while` loops.\n\nA traditional `for` loop is useful when the iteration count or index is important:\n\n```java\nfor (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 3; attempt++) {\n    System.out.println(\"Attempt \" + attempt);\n}\n```\n\nAn enhanced `for` loop iterates over arrays and iterable collections without requiring manual index management:\n\n```java\nString[] environments = {\"dev\", \"test\", \"prod\"};\n\nfor (String environment : environments) {\n    System.out.println(environment);\n}\n```\n\nA `while` loop checks its condition before executing the body. A `do-while` loop executes the body at least once because its condition is checked afterward.\n\n```java\nint remaining = 3;\n\nwhile (remaining > 0) {\n    System.out.println(\"Remaining: \" + remaining);\n    remaining--;\n}\n```\n\nThe `break` statement exits the nearest loop. The `continue` statement skips the remainder of the current iteration and proceeds to the next one. Use them carefully so the loop's termination and processing behavior remain clear.\n\nAn accidental infinite loop can consume CPU and prevent a service from completing work. Always verify that loop conditions can eventually become false. When processing external collections or paginated API results, account for empty responses, repeated pages and changing data.\n\nIn operational code, retry loops should have bounded attempts, appropriate delays and clear failure handling. Do not create an unbounded retry loop around a failing network operation. Use established retry policies with timeouts and backoff when working with distributed services."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Classes, Objects and Methods",
        "content": "# Classes, Objects and Methods\n\nA class defines the structure and behavior of objects. It can declare fields for state, constructors for initialization and methods for operations. An object is a runtime instance created from a class.\n\n```java\npublic class Service {\n    private String name;\n\n    public Service(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n\n    public void start() {\n        System.out.println(name + \" started\");\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreate and use an object:\n\n```java\nService paymentService = new Service(\"payment-api\");\npaymentService.start();\n```\n\nThe `new` operator creates an object and invokes its constructor. The variable `paymentService` stores a reference to that object.\n\nA method defines an operation. Its signature includes the method name and parameter types. A method may return a value or use `void` when it returns no result. Parameters allow callers to provide input, while return values allow methods to provide results.\n\n```java\npublic int calculateTotal(int unitPrice, int quantity) {\n    return unitPrice * quantity;\n}\n```\n\nThe `private` modifier restricts direct access to the field from outside its class. Public methods provide controlled interaction with the object's state.\n\nStatic methods belong to the class rather than a particular object. Instance methods operate in the context of an object and can access its instance state.\n\nKeep classes focused on a clear responsibility. Avoid classes that combine unrelated operations such as HTTP handling, database access, configuration parsing and business rules. Smaller, cohesive classes are easier to test, maintain and troubleshoot."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Packages, Imports and Access Modifiers",
        "content": "# Packages, Imports and Access Modifiers\n\nPackages organize related Java types into namespaces. They help prevent naming conflicts and provide a structure for large applications. A package declaration normally appears at the beginning of a Java source file.\n\n```java\npackage com.example.payment;\n\npublic class PaymentService {\n}\n```\n\nThe package name usually corresponds to a directory structure under the source root:\n\n```text\nsrc/\n  main/\n    java/\n      com/\n        example/\n          payment/\n            PaymentService.java\n```\n\nThe `import` statement allows a type to be referred to by its simple name instead of its fully qualified name.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\n\nList<String> services = new ArrayList<>();\n```\n\nJava provides access modifiers that control where classes and members can be accessed. `public` allows access wherever the declaring type is accessible. `private` restricts access to the declaring class. `protected` allows access within the package and through subclass access rules. A member with no explicit modifier has package-private access.\n\nTop-level classes can be public or package-private. Only one public top-level class is normally declared in a source file, and the filename must match that public class name.\n\nUse packages to reflect application responsibilities, such as controllers, services, repositories, domain models and configuration. Avoid placing every class in one package or creating excessively deep package structures without a clear reason.\n\nAccess modifiers support encapsulation. Keep implementation details private and expose only the operations that other parts of the application genuinely need. This reduces accidental coupling and makes refactoring safer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Strings, Wrapper Classes and Autoboxing",
        "content": "# Strings, Wrapper Classes and Autoboxing\n\nThe `String` class represents text. Strings are immutable: once a String object has been created, its character content cannot be changed. Operations such as concatenation produce a new String value rather than modifying the original object.\n\n```java\nString service = \"payment\";\nString fullName = service + \"-api\";\n\nSystem.out.println(fullName);\n```\n\nUse `equals()` for content comparison:\n\n```java\nString environment = \"production\";\n\nif (\"production\".equals(environment)) {\n    System.out.println(\"Production mode\");\n}\n```\n\nPlacing the known non-null literal first avoids a null dereference if `environment` is null.\n\nFor repeated text construction inside loops, `StringBuilder` is usually more efficient than repeatedly creating intermediate strings:\n\n```java\nStringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n    result.append(\"service-\").append(i).append(\" \");\n}\n\nSystem.out.println(result);\n```\n\nWrapper classes represent primitive values as objects. Examples include `Integer` for `int`, `Long` for `long`, `Double` for `double` and `Boolean` for `boolean`.\n\nAutoboxing converts a primitive into its wrapper representation. Unboxing extracts the primitive value:\n\n```java\nint count = 10;\nInteger boxedCount = count;\n\nint actualCount = boxedCount;\n```\n\nUnboxing a null wrapper reference causes a `NullPointerException`. Validate nullable values before using them as primitives.\n\nWrapper types are necessary in generic collections such as `List<Integer>`. Be aware that wrapper objects can introduce nullability and additional allocation considerations. Use the appropriate type for the application model, compare values correctly and avoid unnecessary conversions in performance-sensitive code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Arrays and Multidimensional Arrays",
        "content": "# Arrays and Multidimensional Arrays\n\nAn array stores a fixed number of elements of the same component type. Its length is determined when the array is created and cannot be changed afterward. Array indexing starts at zero, and the final valid index is `length - 1`.\n\n```java\nint[] ports = {8080, 8081, 9090};\n\nSystem.out.println(ports[0]);\nSystem.out.println(ports.length);\n```\n\nAn array can also be created with a specified size:\n\n```java\nString[] services = new String[3];\nservices[0] = \"api\";\nservices[1] = \"worker\";\nservices[2] = \"scheduler\";\n```\n\nElements receive default values when an array is created. Numeric elements default to zero, boolean elements to false and reference elements to null.\n\nAccessing an invalid index causes `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Check the array length before using an index, especially when the index comes from external input.\n\nA multidimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays:\n\n```java\nint[][] deploymentMatrix = {\n    {1, 0, 1},\n    {1, 1, 0}\n};\n\nSystem.out.println(deploymentMatrix[1][0]);\n```\n\nJava supports arrays with rows of different lengths because each row is a separate array. This is sometimes called a jagged array.\n\nArrays are useful when the size is fixed and indexed access is required. When the number of elements changes dynamically, collections such as `ArrayList` are often more suitable.\n\nArrays are objects and can be passed to methods by passing their references. A method that changes an array element can affect the same array observed by the caller. Consider this shared mutable state when designing methods and tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-13",
        "title": "Java Development and Execution Workflow",
        "content": "# Java Development and Execution Workflow\n\nA Java development workflow moves source code through editing, compilation, testing, packaging and execution. A developer typically writes source files under a source directory, declares dependencies, compiles the code and runs automated tests before packaging an application.\n\nA simple command-line workflow is:\n\n```bash\njavac -d out src/HelloJava.java\njava -cp out HelloJava\n```\n\nReal applications usually have multiple classes and external libraries. Build tools such as Maven and Gradle provide dependency management, standard directory layouts, build lifecycles, test execution and artifact packaging.\n\nA typical Maven workflow includes:\n\n```bash\nmvn clean\nmvn test\nmvn package\n```\n\n`clean` removes generated build output. `test` compiles and executes tests. `package` creates the configured distributable artifact after the required earlier lifecycle phases.\n\nA CI pipeline should use a controlled JDK version, restore dependencies from approved repositories, compile the source, execute automated tests, inspect test results and publish the packaged artifact. Build failures should stop promotion rather than silently allowing an unverified artifact to reach production.\n\nThe runtime environment then launches the packaged application using a compatible Java runtime. Configuration such as ports, database addresses and credentials should be supplied through controlled environment-specific configuration rather than hardcoded into source code.\n\nKeep build logs, test reports, artifact checksums and version information. These records help identify which source revision and build produced a deployed application. Reproducible build inputs and immutable artifacts make release investigation and rollback more reliable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-14",
        "title": "Java Language Troubleshooting and Best Practices",
        "content": "# Java Language Troubleshooting and Best Practices\n\nJava failures can occur during compilation, application startup or runtime execution. Identify the failure stage before changing code or configuration.\n\nCompilation errors often include invalid syntax, missing imports, incompatible types, inaccessible members or unsupported language features. Check the compiler output and confirm that the configured JDK supports the source version used by the project.\n\nRuntime exceptions occur after the application starts executing. Common examples include `NullPointerException`, `NumberFormatException`, `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` and `ClassCastException`. Read the exception type, message and stack trace. The first application-owned stack frame often points toward the code that needs investigation.\n\nA missing class at runtime may produce `ClassNotFoundException` or `NoClassDefFoundError`. Check whether the required dependency is included in the packaged artifact and available through the runtime classpath or module path.\n\nUseful checks include:\n\n```bash\njava -version\njavac -version\njar --list --file application.jar\n```\n\nFor a packaged application, inspect the archive contents and verify that the required classes and resources are present. Compare the Java version, startup command, environment variables and dependency versions between a working environment and the failing environment.\n\nFollow these practices:\n\n- Use meaningful class, method and variable names.\n- Keep methods focused and avoid unnecessary complexity.\n- Validate external input and configuration.\n- Prefer explicit types and clear control flow.\n- Use `equals()` for object content comparison.\n- Handle exceptions at appropriate application boundaries.\n- Avoid swallowing exceptions without logging or recovery.\n- Write tests for normal behavior, invalid input and boundary conditions.\n- Keep Java versions and dependencies controlled in CI/CD.\n- Never hardcode production credentials in source code.\n\nA useful troubleshooting process reproduces the problem, captures the exact error, identifies the failing layer, verifies assumptions and tests the correction. Avoid changing multiple unrelated settings at once because that makes the actual cause harder to identify."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Project Structure",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Java Project Layout and Directory Conventions",
        "content": "# Java Project Layout and Directory Conventions\n\nA Java project layout defines where source code, tests, configuration files, documentation and generated artifacts are stored. A consistent layout makes a project easier to understand, build, test, review and maintain. It also allows build tools and IDEs to discover files using predictable conventions.\n\nA typical Maven-style Java project uses this structure:\n\n```text\npayment-service/\n├── pom.xml\n├── src/\n│   ├── main/\n│   │   ├── java/\n│   │   │   └── com/example/payment/\n│   │   │       └── PaymentApplication.java\n│   │   └── resources/\n│   │       └── application.yml\n│   └── test/\n│       ├── java/\n│       │   └── com/example/payment/\n│       └── resources/\n├── target/\n└── README.md\n```\n\nThe `src/main/java` directory contains application source code. `src/main/resources` contains files needed by the application, such as properties, YAML configuration, templates and static resources. `src/test/java` contains automated test source code. `src/test/resources` contains test-specific input files and configuration. The `target` directory is generated by Maven and normally should not be committed to source control.\n\nThe root directory contains project-level files such as `pom.xml`, `.gitignore`, README documentation and build configuration. A project may also contain deployment manifests, Dockerfiles and CI pipeline definitions.\n\nThe standard layout is a convention, not a requirement of the Java language. Build tools can be configured to use alternative directories, but following established conventions reduces custom configuration and makes onboarding easier.\n\nFor DevOps teams, predictable project layout helps CI systems locate source files, execute tests, package resources and publish artifacts consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Source Roots and Resource Directories",
        "content": "# Source Roots and Resource Directories\n\nA source root is a directory that a build tool treats as the starting point for discovering source files. In a standard Maven project, `src/main/java` is the main Java source root and `src/test/java` is the test source root.\n\nJava files under a source root are compiled into class files. Their package declarations determine their logical namespace and normally correspond to subdirectories below the source root.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nsrc/main/java/\n└── com/\n    └── example/\n        └── payment/\n            └── PaymentService.java\n```\n\nThe source file should declare:\n\n```java\npackage com.example.payment;\n```\n\nResources are different from Java source files. Files under `src/main/resources` are copied into the application's output so they can be loaded at runtime. Examples include `application.properties`, `application.yml`, logging configuration, message bundles and static templates.\n\nTest resources belong under `src/test/resources`. They may contain sample JSON, SQL initialization scripts, certificates for controlled tests or test configuration. They should not accidentally be included as production application resources.\n\nMaven's standard resource and source directory conventions allow a project to build without manually specifying every directory. If a project uses non-standard paths, configure them explicitly in the build file and verify that the intended files are included.\n\nA common mistake is placing configuration files in the repository root and assuming they will automatically be available inside the packaged JAR. They are not necessarily copied as application resources. Put runtime classpath resources in the appropriate resources directory or explicitly configure how they are packaged.\n\nInspect the generated output after building. Confirm that required configuration files and compiled classes exist in the expected locations. This catches missing-resource problems before deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Package Naming and Namespace Organization",
        "content": "# Package Naming and Namespace Organization\n\nJava packages organize classes and interfaces into namespaces. They prevent unrelated classes from having to share one global class name and provide a logical structure for application code.\n\nA package declaration appears at the beginning of a Java source file:\n\n```java\npackage com.example.payment.service;\n\npublic class PaymentService {\n}\n```\n\nThe corresponding directory structure normally looks like:\n\n```text\nsrc/main/java/com/example/payment/service/PaymentService.java\n```\n\nPackage names are conventionally written in lowercase. Organizations commonly use a reversed domain name, such as `com.example`, as the beginning of their package namespace. This reduces the possibility of collisions with classes from other organizations.\n\nA layered application might use packages such as:\n\n```text\ncom.example.payment\n├── controller\n├── service\n├── repository\n├── domain\n├── dto\n└── config\n```\n\nThe controller package handles incoming requests, the service package contains application operations, the repository package handles persistence access, and the domain package represents core business concepts. DTO packages contain request or response data structures, while config contains application configuration classes.\n\nPackage organization should reflect actual responsibilities. Do not create dozens of empty packages just to make the directory tree look complex. Also avoid placing unrelated classes into one large package.\n\nPackages are not a replacement for access control or architecture enforcement. Classes in the same package can access package-private members, so package boundaries should be designed deliberately.\n\nIn large applications, clear package organization helps developers identify where a change belongs, reduces accidental dependencies and supports code review. Build and test teams can also use package conventions when configuring test discovery and static analysis."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Main Application Entry Point",
        "content": "# Main Application Entry Point\n\nA standalone Java application commonly starts through a class that exposes a `public static void main(String[] args)` method. The Java launcher loads the specified class and invokes this method as the application's entry point.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\npackage com.example.payment;\n\npublic class PaymentApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Payment application started\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe method components have specific meanings. `public` makes the method accessible to the launcher. `static` means it can be invoked without creating an instance of the class. `void` means it does not return a value. `String[] args` receives command-line arguments.\n\nCompile and launch a simple application:\n\n```bash\njavac -d out src/main/java/com/example/payment/PaymentApplication.java\njava -cp out com.example.payment.PaymentApplication\n```\n\nThe fully qualified class name includes the package. If the package is omitted from the launch command, the JVM may fail to locate the class.\n\nIn a Spring Boot project, the main class commonly delegates startup to Spring:\n\n```java\n@SpringBootApplication\npublic class PaymentApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(PaymentApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe annotation and framework startup call are Spring Boot features; the underlying Java entry point remains the `main` method.\n\nKeep the main class focused on application startup. Business logic, database operations and request handling should live in their appropriate components rather than being placed inside `main`.\n\nWhen troubleshooting startup, verify the configured main class, package name, build artifact manifest and Java launch command. A missing or incorrect entry point can prevent an otherwise successfully compiled application from launching."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Test Source and Test Resource Organization",
        "content": "# Test Source and Test Resource Organization\n\nJava projects separate production code from automated test code so that test-only classes and dependencies do not become part of the production application unintentionally.\n\nIn a Maven project, production source code belongs under `src/main/java`, while test source code belongs under `src/test/java`. Test input files and test configuration belong under `src/test/resources`.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nsrc/\n├── main/\n│   ├── java/com/example/payment/\n│   │   └── PaymentService.java\n│   └── resources/\n│       └── application.yml\n└── test/\n    ├── java/com/example/payment/\n    │   └── PaymentServiceTest.java\n    └── resources/\n        └── payment-test-data.json\n```\n\nA unit test might verify a calculation without starting a database or web server:\n\n```java\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\n\nclass PaymentServiceTest {\n    @Test\n    void calculatesTotal() {\n        PaymentService service = new PaymentService();\n        assertEquals(200, service.calculateTotal(100, 2));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe test framework discovers and executes the test based on its configuration and naming conventions. Maven's test lifecycle uses the configured test provider and plugins to compile and run these tests.\n\nKeep test data in test resources rather than hardcoding large fixtures throughout test methods. Use separate test configuration for database URLs, mock endpoints and other test-only settings. Never put production secrets into test resources or commit real credentials.\n\nA CI pipeline should compile test sources and execute the test suite before packaging or publishing the application. Review test reports and ensure that a failing test causes the pipeline to fail. Clear separation between production and test directories makes the build more predictable and prevents test-only files from leaking into production artifacts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Build Files and Project Metadata",
        "content": "# Build Files and Project Metadata\n\nA Java build file describes how a project is compiled, tested, packaged and connected to external libraries. Maven uses a Project Object Model file named `pom.xml`. Gradle commonly uses `build.gradle` or `build.gradle.kts`.\n\nA minimal Maven project definition looks like:\n\n```xml\n<project xmlns=\"http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0\"\n         xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\"\n         xsi:schemaLocation=\"http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0\n         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd\">\n    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>\n\n    <groupId>com.example</groupId>\n    <artifactId>payment-service</artifactId>\n    <version>1.0.0</version>\n\n    <properties>\n        <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>\n        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>\n    </properties>\n</project>\n```\n\nThe `groupId` identifies the organization or project namespace. The `artifactId` identifies the project artifact, and `version` identifies its release version. Compiler properties define the Java release targeted by the build.\n\nA build file may also declare dependencies, plugins, repositories, profiles and packaging behavior. Keep versions controlled and avoid relying on unspecified defaults for important build behavior.\n\nProject metadata is used by developers, IDEs, build servers and artifact repositories. Incorrect coordinates can cause publication collisions or make dependencies difficult to locate. Inconsistent compiler settings can also produce code that builds on one machine but fails on another.\n\nUse source control to track build files and review changes to plugin or dependency versions. CI should build from the committed build definition rather than depending on undocumented settings installed on a developer's machine."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Dependencies and External Libraries",
        "content": "# Dependencies and External Libraries\n\nA dependency is an external library or module that a Java project requires to compile, test or run. Examples include logging libraries, JSON serializers, database drivers, testing frameworks and application frameworks.\n\nMaven declares dependencies in `pom.xml`:\n\n```xml\n<dependencies>\n    <dependency>\n        <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>\n        <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>\n        <version>2.0.16</version>\n    </dependency>\n\n    <dependency>\n        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>\n        <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>\n        <version>5.11.0</version>\n        <scope>test</scope>\n    </dependency>\n</dependencies>\n```\n\nA dependency is identified by coordinates such as group ID, artifact ID and version. Maven resolves required artifacts from configured repositories and stores downloaded files in its local repository.\n\nDependency scope determines where a dependency is available. For example, `test` dependencies are used for test compilation and execution rather than normal production compilation. Other scopes have different effects on classpaths and transitive dependency propagation.\n\nA transitive dependency is a library required by another dependency. This can create version conflicts or introduce libraries that the project did not declare directly. Inspect the resolved dependency tree when unexpected behavior occurs:\n\n```bash\nmvn dependency:tree\n```\n\nDo not add arbitrary JAR files manually to a project without documenting how they are obtained and versioned. Prefer managed dependencies with explicit versions and approved repositories.\n\nIn CI/CD, dependency resolution should be repeatable and monitored for known vulnerabilities. Teams should review dependency updates, use trusted repositories and retain enough build information to identify which library versions were included in a released artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Compiled Classes, JARs and Build Output",
        "content": "# Compiled Classes, JARs and Build Output\n\nJava build output contains generated files produced during compilation, testing and packaging. In a Maven project, the default output directory is `target`.\n\nA typical build directory may contain:\n\n```text\ntarget/\n├── classes/\n├── test-classes/\n├── surefire-reports/\n└── payment-service-1.0.0.jar\n```\n\nThe `classes` directory contains compiled production classes and copied resources. The `test-classes` directory contains compiled test classes and test resources. Test reports are generated by the configured test plugin. The JAR is the packaged application artifact.\n\nA JAR is an archive containing Java class files, resources and metadata. Inspect its contents with:\n\n```bash\njar --list --file target/payment-service-1.0.0.jar\n```\n\nAn executable JAR typically has manifest metadata identifying its entry point or framework launcher. Launch behavior depends on how the artifact was packaged:\n\n```bash\njava -jar target/payment-service-1.0.0.jar\n```\n\nNot every JAR is executable with `java -jar`. A library JAR may have no application entry point and may need to be used as a dependency by another application.\n\nGenerated output should normally not be committed to source control. Add build directories such as `target/` to `.gitignore`. The CI pipeline should generate output from the checked-out source and controlled dependencies.\n\nFor reliable deployments, publish the artifact produced by the successful build. Record its version and checksum, and promote that same artifact through environments. Do not assume that a JAR with the same filename necessarily contains identical bytes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Multi-Module Java Project Organization",
        "content": "# Multi-Module Java Project Organization\n\nA multi-module Java project separates a larger system into smaller build modules. Each module can own a specific responsibility, dependency set and artifact while sharing a parent build configuration.\n\nA Maven multi-module repository commonly looks like:\n\n```text\npayment-platform/\n├── pom.xml\n├── payment-domain/\n│   └── pom.xml\n├── payment-service/\n│   └── pom.xml\n└── payment-api/\n    └── pom.xml\n```\n\nThe root POM can act as an aggregator and list modules:\n\n```xml\n<packaging>pom</packaging>\n\n<modules>\n    <module>payment-domain</module>\n    <module>payment-service</module>\n    <module>payment-api</module>\n</modules>\n```\n\nThe domain module may contain core business types. The service module may implement application operations and depend on the domain module. The API module may expose HTTP endpoints and depend on the service module.\n\nA parent POM can centralize shared properties, plugin management and dependency version management. A module POM declares its own artifact coordinates and dependencies. Aggregation and inheritance are related but distinct Maven concepts: an aggregator lists modules to build together, while a parent provides inherited configuration.\n\nBuild the project from the root:\n\n```bash\nmvn clean verify\n```\n\nMaven calculates the required module build order from inter-module dependencies. If a module depends on another module, the required dependency is built first.\n\nMulti-module design is useful when components have meaningful boundaries and can be tested or reused independently. Avoid splitting a small application into many modules without a clear architectural reason. Excessive module boundaries can increase build complexity and slow down development.\n\nCI pipelines should build the required module set, run relevant tests and publish the correct artifacts. Changes to shared modules may affect downstream modules, so dependency relationships must be understood before release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Project Structure Validation and Troubleshooting",
        "content": "# Project Structure Validation and Troubleshooting\n\nProject structure problems often appear as compilation failures, missing resources, test discovery failures or runtime class-loading errors. Validate the directory layout and build configuration before changing application code.\n\nStart by checking the repository contents and build file:\n\n```bash\nmvn validate\nmvn test\nmvn package\n```\n\nThe `validate` phase checks basic project information and configuration. `test` compiles and runs tests. `package` creates the configured artifact after the required earlier lifecycle phases.\n\nCommon problems include:\n\n- Java source files placed outside configured source roots.\n- Package declarations that do not match the intended namespace layout.\n- Configuration files stored outside resource directories.\n- Test classes placed under production source directories.\n- Dependencies missing from the build file.\n- Incorrect module declarations in a multi-module project.\n- Generated output accidentally committed to source control.\n- A main class missing from the executable artifact metadata.\n\nIf a resource is missing at runtime, inspect the packaged JAR rather than checking only the source tree:\n\n```bash\njar --list --file target/payment-service-1.0.0.jar\n```\n\nIf a test is not discovered, verify its source directory, test framework dependency, naming convention and test plugin configuration.\n\nIf a module cannot resolve another project module, inspect the root aggregator POM, module paths, artifact coordinates and declared dependencies.\n\nUse a clean build when stale generated output may be hiding a problem:\n\n```bash\nmvn clean verify\n```\n\nA clean build removes previous generated output and rebuilds the project. In CI, build from a fresh checkout where practical and retain test reports and build logs.\n\nA well-structured project makes failures easier to isolate. Validate source roots, resource packaging, module dependencies and artifact contents before concluding that the Java code itself is defective."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Object-Oriented Programming",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Object-Oriented Programming Principles",
        "content": "# Object-Oriented Programming Principles\n\nObject-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming approach that organizes software around objects containing state and behavior. A class defines the structure and operations of a type, while an object is a runtime instance of that class. OOP helps represent application concepts such as customers, payments, orders, deployment jobs and infrastructure resources.\n\nFour commonly taught OOP principles are encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction.\n\nEncapsulation protects an object's internal state and provides controlled methods for interacting with it. Inheritance allows a class to reuse and specialize behavior from another class. Polymorphism allows different implementations to be used through a common parent type or interface. Abstraction exposes essential operations while hiding implementation details.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nclass Deployment {\n    private String environment;\n    private boolean completed;\n\n    public Deployment(String environment) {\n        this.environment = environment;\n    }\n\n    public void complete() {\n        completed = true;\n    }\n\n    public boolean isCompleted() {\n        return completed;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `Deployment` class owns its state and provides an operation to change it. Other classes do not directly modify the private fields.\n\nOOP is not simply a requirement to create many classes. It is useful when objects have meaningful responsibilities, state transitions and relationships. A poorly designed object model can introduce unnecessary complexity, excessive inheritance and tightly coupled components.\n\nIn backend and DevOps-related applications, OOP is commonly used to model business operations, configuration objects, API services and reusable infrastructure components. Good OOP design improves testability and maintainability by keeping responsibilities clear and dependencies controlled."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Classes, Objects and Object State",
        "content": "# Classes, Objects and Object State\n\nA class is a definition that describes the fields, constructors and methods available on objects of that type. An object is a concrete instance created at runtime. Each ordinary object has its own instance state, although static members belong to the class and are shared.\n\n```java\nclass Server {\n    private String hostname;\n    private int port;\n\n    public Server(String hostname, int port) {\n        this.hostname = hostname;\n        this.port = port;\n    }\n\n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(hostname + \":\" + port);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreate two independent objects:\n\n```java\nServer apiServer = new Server(\"api.example.com\", 8080);\nServer workerServer = new Server(\"worker.example.com\", 9090);\n\napiServer.display();\nworkerServer.display();\n```\n\nBoth objects use the same class definition, but their instance fields hold different values.\n\nObject state consists of the values stored in an object's fields at a particular time. A method can change that state if the class permits the operation. For example, a deployment object may move from `PENDING` to `RUNNING` and then to `COMPLETED`.\n\nA reference variable refers to an object. Assigning one reference variable to another does not automatically clone the object:\n\n```java\nServer first = new Server(\"api.example.com\", 8080);\nServer second = first;\n```\n\nBoth variables refer to the same object. This matters when mutable state is shared across application components.\n\nUse constructors to establish valid initial state. Keep fields private when direct access would allow invalid values. Define methods that represent meaningful operations rather than exposing arbitrary state changes.\n\nWhen debugging object behavior, inspect whether two variables refer to the same object, which methods can modify its state and whether multiple threads can access it concurrently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Encapsulation and Data Hiding",
        "content": "# Encapsulation and Data Hiding\n\nEncapsulation combines data and the operations that manage that data inside a class. Data hiding restricts direct access to implementation details, commonly through Java access modifiers such as `private`.\n\nWithout encapsulation, callers can assign invalid values directly:\n\n```java\nclass UnsafeAccount {\n    public double balance;\n}\n```\n\nAny caller could assign a negative balance without validation. A safer design controls state changes through methods:\n\n```java\nclass Account {\n    private long balanceInPaise;\n\n    public Account(long openingBalanceInPaise) {\n        if (openingBalanceInPaise < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Opening balance cannot be negative\");\n        }\n        this.balanceInPaise = openingBalanceInPaise;\n    }\n\n    public void deposit(long amountInPaise) {\n        if (amountInPaise <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Deposit must be positive\");\n        }\n        balanceInPaise += amountInPaise;\n    }\n\n    public long getBalanceInPaise() {\n        return balanceInPaise;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe field is private, and the deposit method enforces a business rule. The caller cannot bypass that rule through ordinary direct field access.\n\nEncapsulation does not mean every field must always have a getter and setter. A setter that accepts any value may expose the same risks as a public field. Provide only operations that make sense for the object's responsibility.\n\nData hiding also reduces coupling. Other classes depend on the public contract rather than the internal representation. The implementation can later change from one field to another without requiring every caller to change.\n\nIn production systems, encapsulation helps protect important invariants such as valid order states, payment amounts, retry limits and configuration values. Validate input at appropriate boundaries and keep object invariants enforced inside the class that owns the state."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "Inheritance and Method Overriding",
        "content": "# Inheritance and Method Overriding\n\nInheritance allows a Java class to extend another class and reuse accessible fields and methods. The existing class is called the superclass, and the derived class is called the subclass. Java supports single class inheritance: a class can directly extend only one class.\n\n```java\nclass Notification {\n    public void send() {\n        System.out.println(\"Sending notification\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass EmailNotification extends Notification {\n    @Override\n    public void send() {\n        System.out.println(\"Sending email notification\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`EmailNotification` inherits from `Notification` and overrides its `send()` method with specialized behavior. The `@Override` annotation asks the compiler to verify that the method really overrides an inherited method.\n\nA subclass can invoke an accessible superclass implementation with `super`:\n\n```java\nclass EmailNotification extends Notification {\n    @Override\n    public void send() {\n        super.send();\n        System.out.println(\"Email delivery started\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nConstructors are not inherited as ordinary methods. A subclass constructor can invoke a superclass constructor through `super(...)`. If no explicit superclass constructor invocation is supplied, Java attempts to invoke an accessible no-argument superclass constructor.\n\nInheritance should model a genuine \"is-a\" relationship. An email notification is a kind of notification, so the example is reasonable. A database connection is not a kind of logging service merely because both are used by an application.\n\nUse inheritance when shared behavior and substitutability are meaningful. Avoid deep inheritance hierarchies that make behavior difficult to trace. Composition and interfaces are often better choices when a class needs to use another component without being a specialized version of it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "Polymorphism and Dynamic Method Dispatch",
        "content": "# Polymorphism and Dynamic Method Dispatch\n\nPolymorphism allows code to work with a common parent type while the actual object provides its own implementation. With runtime polymorphism, an overridden instance method is selected according to the actual object type at execution time. This behavior is called dynamic method dispatch.\n\n```java\nclass Notification {\n    public void send() {\n        System.out.println(\"Generic notification\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass EmailNotification extends Notification {\n    @Override\n    public void send() {\n        System.out.println(\"Email sent\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass SmsNotification extends Notification {\n    @Override\n    public void send() {\n        System.out.println(\"SMS sent\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nA caller can use the common parent type:\n\n```java\nNotification first = new EmailNotification();\nNotification second = new SmsNotification();\n\nfirst.send();\nsecond.send();\n```\n\nAlthough both variables are declared as `Notification`, the JVM invokes the overridden method belonging to the actual object. The first call prints the email behavior, and the second prints the SMS behavior.\n\nPolymorphism reduces the need for callers to know every implementation class. A notification service can accept a `Notification` reference and delegate the sending operation without containing separate logic for every delivery type.\n\nJava also supports compile-time polymorphism through method overloading. Overloaded methods share a name but have different parameter lists. Overload selection is determined by compile-time method resolution, not by runtime overriding.\n\nDo not confuse an object's declared reference type with its runtime class. The declared type controls which members can be accessed through the reference, while dynamic dispatch selects an applicable overridden instance method.\n\nPolymorphism is useful for payment providers, storage implementations, notification channels and deployment strategies. It allows new implementations to be introduced while keeping callers dependent on stable contracts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Abstraction and Abstract Classes",
        "content": "# Abstraction and Abstract Classes\n\nAbstraction exposes the essential operations of a component while hiding the details of how those operations are performed. It allows callers to focus on what a component does rather than every internal step.\n\nJava supports abstraction through interfaces and abstract classes. An abstract class is declared with the `abstract` keyword and cannot be instantiated directly. It can contain abstract methods without implementations and concrete methods with implementations.\n\n```java\nabstract class BuildTask {\n    public void start() {\n        System.out.println(\"Build task started\");\n    }\n\n    public abstract void execute();\n}\n```\n\nA subclass must implement inherited abstract methods unless the subclass is also abstract:\n\n```java\nclass MavenBuildTask extends BuildTask {\n    @Override\n    public void execute() {\n        System.out.println(\"Executing Maven build\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe abstract class provides shared startup behavior, while the concrete subclass defines the specific execution operation.\n\nAn abstract class can contain instance fields, constructors, static methods and concrete methods. This makes it useful when related subclasses share state or common implementation.\n\nAbstraction is valuable when application code should depend on a stable operation rather than a specific implementation. For example, a build orchestration service may work with a general build task while Maven and Gradle implementations provide different execution details.\n\nDo not make every class abstract. Use an abstract class when there is a meaningful common base and shared implementation. If unrelated implementations only need to follow the same contract, an interface is usually more appropriate."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "Interfaces and Contract-Based Design",
        "content": "# Interfaces and Contract-Based Design\n\nAn interface defines a contract that implementing classes agree to provide. It describes available operations without requiring callers to depend on a particular implementation class.\n\n```java\ninterface ArtifactPublisher {\n    void publish(String artifactPath);\n}\n```\n\nDifferent implementations can provide different publishing mechanisms:\n\n```java\nclass NexusPublisher implements ArtifactPublisher {\n    @Override\n    public void publish(String artifactPath) {\n        System.out.println(\"Publishing to Nexus: \" + artifactPath);\n    }\n}\n\nclass CloudPublisher implements ArtifactPublisher {\n    @Override\n    public void publish(String artifactPath) {\n        System.out.println(\"Publishing to cloud storage: \" + artifactPath);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nA caller can depend on the interface:\n\n```java\nclass ReleaseService {\n    private final ArtifactPublisher publisher;\n\n    ReleaseService(ArtifactPublisher publisher) {\n        this.publisher = publisher;\n    }\n\n    void release(String path) {\n        publisher.publish(path);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe release service does not need to know the internal details of Nexus or cloud storage. It only needs an object that fulfills the publishing contract.\n\nJava interfaces can contain abstract methods, default methods, static methods and private helper methods subject to language rules. A class can implement multiple interfaces, which allows it to satisfy several independent contracts without multiple class inheritance.\n\nInterfaces are useful for testing because a test can supply a fake or mock implementation. They also support dependency inversion by allowing high-level application logic to depend on abstractions rather than concrete infrastructure classes.\n\nDesign interfaces around meaningful responsibilities. Avoid enormous interfaces containing unrelated methods, and avoid creating an interface for every class without a real need for multiple implementations or a useful abstraction boundary."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-8",
        "title": "Constructors, Initialization and Object Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Constructors, Initialization and Object Lifecycle\n\nA constructor initializes a new object. Its name matches the class name, and it has no return type. A constructor can accept parameters so that the object begins with valid state.\n\n```java\nclass Deployment {\n    private final String application;\n    private final String environment;\n\n    public Deployment(String application, String environment) {\n        this.application = application;\n        this.environment = environment;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreate an object using the `new` operator:\n\n```java\nDeployment deployment =\n    new Deployment(\"payment-api\", \"staging\");\n```\n\nJava performs class initialization and object initialization according to defined rules. Static fields and static initialization blocks are processed as the class is initialized. During object creation, instance fields and instance initializer blocks are processed as part of initialization, followed by constructor execution. Superclass initialization occurs before subclass initialization.\n\nA constructor can call another constructor in the same class through `this(...)`, or a superclass constructor through `super(...)`. Such constructor invocation must appear first in the constructor body under traditional Java syntax. Modern Java releases also support additional restricted constructor-body features, so projects should follow their configured language version.\n\nIf a class declares no constructor, the compiler may provide a default no-argument constructor. Once a constructor is explicitly declared, that automatic default constructor is not supplied.\n\nJava does not provide a general-purpose deterministic destructor. Objects become eligible for garbage collection when they are no longer reachable, but garbage collection timing is not a resource-cleanup guarantee. Use `try-with-resources` for resources such as files, sockets and database connections that implement `AutoCloseable`.\n\nGood constructors establish object invariants. Avoid constructors that perform slow network calls or complex external operations because they make object creation difficult to test and control."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-9",
        "title": "Composition, Aggregation and Association",
        "content": "# Composition, Aggregation and Association\n\nObjects often collaborate with other objects. Association describes a general relationship in which one object knows about or uses another. Aggregation and composition describe more specific whole-part relationships.\n\nIn composition, a containing object owns a component as part of its internal structure. The component is generally treated as part of the containing object's design and lifecycle.\n\n```java\nclass Engine {\n    void start() {\n        System.out.println(\"Engine started\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass ApplicationServer {\n    private final Engine engine = new Engine();\n\n    void startServer() {\n        engine.start();\n        System.out.println(\"Server started\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe server creates and owns its engine in this simplified example.\n\nAggregation is a weaker whole-part relationship. The related object can exist independently and may be shared or supplied from outside:\n\n```java\nclass Team {\n    private final List<Developer> developers;\n\n    Team(List<Developer> developers) {\n        this.developers = developers;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `Developer` objects are created outside the `Team` and can exist independently of it.\n\nAssociation is broader than either relationship. A service may use a logger, repository or HTTP client without owning the lifecycle of those components.\n\nComposition is often preferred over inheritance when a class needs another component's behavior but is not a specialized form of that component. It keeps responsibilities separate and allows components to be replaced or tested independently.\n\nFor production systems, explicit object relationships help determine ownership, lifecycle management and dependency boundaries. Avoid unclear ownership of mutable objects, shared resources or connections. Make it clear which component creates, configures, uses and closes each resource."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-10",
        "title": "SOLID Principles and Maintainable Design",
        "content": "# SOLID Principles and Maintainable Design\n\nSOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles intended to improve maintainability and reduce unnecessary coupling.\n\n**Single Responsibility Principle:** A class should have one coherent responsibility and one main reason to change. For example, a class that validates payments, sends email and writes database records has unrelated responsibilities. Separate those operations into focused components.\n\n**Open/Closed Principle:** Software components should be open to extension but closed to unnecessary modification. A notification interface with separate email and SMS implementations allows another channel to be introduced without rewriting the caller.\n\n**Liskov Substitution Principle:** A subtype should be usable wherever its parent type is expected without breaking the parent's behavioral expectations. A subclass should not unexpectedly reject valid operations that callers rely on.\n\n**Interface Segregation Principle:** Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. Prefer focused interfaces over one large interface that combines unrelated operations.\n\n**Dependency Inversion Principle:** High-level policy should depend on abstractions rather than concrete infrastructure details. For example, a release service can depend on an artifact-publisher interface rather than constructing a specific repository client internally.\n\nA simple dependency-inversion example:\n\n```java\ninterface DeploymentTarget {\n    void deploy(String artifact);\n}\n\nclass ReleaseManager {\n    private final DeploymentTarget target;\n\n    ReleaseManager(DeploymentTarget target) {\n        this.target = target;\n    }\n\n    void release(String artifact) {\n        target.deploy(artifact);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe release manager can work with different deployment target implementations.\n\nSOLID is guidance, not a requirement to introduce layers, interfaces or factories everywhere. Excessive abstraction can make a small application harder to understand. Apply the principles when they solve a real problem such as duplicated behavior, difficult testing, unstable dependencies or frequent unrelated changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-11",
        "title": "Object Equality, Immutability and Design Safety",
        "content": "# Object Equality, Immutability and Design Safety\n\nJava objects have identity and may also have logical equality. The `==` operator checks whether two reference variables refer to the same object. The `equals()` method is used to define logical equality when a class needs value-based comparison.\n\nWhen overriding `equals()`, a class should also override `hashCode()` consistently. Equal objects must produce equal hash codes. This contract is essential for correct behavior in hash-based collections such as `HashMap` and `HashSet`.\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nimport java.util.Objects;\n\nclass ServiceKey {\n    private final String name;\n\n    ServiceKey(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object other) {\n        if (this == other) return true;\n        if (!(other instanceof ServiceKey)) return false;\n        ServiceKey that = (ServiceKey) other;\n        return Objects.equals(name, that.name);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(name);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAn immutable object cannot have its observable state changed after construction. Common techniques include private final fields, no setters, defensive copying of mutable inputs and returning safe representations rather than exposing internal mutable objects.\n\nImmutability reduces accidental state changes and makes objects easier to share between components. It can also simplify concurrent programming because immutable state does not require synchronization for ordinary reads.\n\nA final reference does not automatically make the referenced object immutable. A final field containing a mutable list can still refer to a list whose contents change.\n\nUse immutable value objects for configuration, identifiers and other data that should not change after creation. For mutable entities, define clear state transitions and protect invariants. Test equality, hash-code behavior and defensive copying when those features are part of the class contract."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-12",
        "title": "OOP Implementation Exercise and Design Validation",
        "content": "# OOP Implementation Exercise and Design Validation\n\nThis exercise combines classes, encapsulation, interfaces, composition and polymorphism in a small deployment model.\n\nFirst define a deployment contract:\n\n```java\ninterface DeploymentExecutor {\n    void execute(String application, String environment);\n}\n```\n\nCreate an implementation:\n\n```java\nclass LocalDeploymentExecutor implements DeploymentExecutor {\n    @Override\n    public void execute(String application, String environment) {\n        System.out.println(\n            \"Deploying \" + application + \" to \" + environment\n        );\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCreate a service that uses the abstraction:\n\n```java\nclass DeploymentService {\n    private final DeploymentExecutor executor;\n\n    DeploymentService(DeploymentExecutor executor) {\n        this.executor = executor;\n    }\n\n    public void deploy(String application, String environment) {\n        if (application == null || application.isBlank()) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Application is required\");\n        }\n\n        if (environment == null || environment.isBlank()) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Environment is required\");\n        }\n\n        executor.execute(application, environment);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe service validates its inputs and delegates the actual execution to the injected implementation. It does not need to know whether deployment happens locally, through a cloud provider or through a container platform.\n\nA simple caller can create and use these objects:\n\n```java\nDeploymentExecutor executor = new LocalDeploymentExecutor();\nDeploymentService service = new DeploymentService(executor);\n\nservice.deploy(\"payment-api\", \"staging\");\n```\n\nValidate the design by checking:\n\n1. Is each class responsible for one coherent task?\n2. Is important internal state private?\n3. Are invalid values rejected at the correct boundary?\n4. Can another executor implementation be supplied without rewriting the service?\n5. Can the service be tested with a fake executor?\n6. Are object creation and resource ownership clear?\n\nAdd tests for valid deployment input, missing application names, missing environments and executor behavior. A fake implementation can record the arguments it receives so the service can be tested without a real deployment system.\n\nThis exercise demonstrates how OOP principles support modular application design. In a production implementation, deployment execution would also need authorization, audit logging, timeouts, error handling and safe handling of credentials."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Collections and Generics",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "Collections and Generics — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Collections and Generics — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nThe Java Collections Framework provides standard interfaces and implementations for storing, retrieving, ordering, and processing groups of objects. Generics add compile-time type safety and reduce unsafe casts.\n\nThis section focuses on Establish the language or platform fundamentals required to understand this subject. The explanation is specific to **Collections and Generics** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nList implementations preserve positional order, Set implementations enforce uniqueness, and Map implementations associate keys with values. ArrayList uses a resizable array, HashMap uses hash-based buckets, and TreeMap maintains keys in sorted order. Generic type parameters are primarily enforced by the compiler through type erasure.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA monitoring application can store alert events in a List, track unique service identifiers in a Set, and map service names to health status in a Map. Choosing the collection according to lookup, ordering, and uniqueness requirements affects performance and correctness.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse List<Deployment> for ordered deployment records, Set<String> for unique environment names, and Map<String, DeploymentStatus> for status lookup. Compare ArrayList and HashSet membership behavior, and test duplicate values, missing keys, iteration, and null handling.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace one small example from its initial input through the relevant language or framework operations to its observable result.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nFrequent errors include modifying a collection while iterating, using mutable map keys, assuming HashMap iteration order, selecting a list for repeated membership checks, ignoring null handling, and using raw types that bypass generic type checking.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the relevant terminology, execution model, core abstractions, and the relationship between source code and runtime behavior. Clarify what the developer configures and what the platform manages automatically.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "Collections and Generics — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Collections and Generics — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nThe Java Collections Framework provides standard interfaces and implementations for storing, retrieving, ordering, and processing groups of objects. Generics add compile-time type safety and reduce unsafe casts.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the internal concepts that determine how this subject behaves. The explanation is specific to **Collections and Generics** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nList implementations preserve positional order, Set implementations enforce uniqueness, and Map implementations associate keys with values. ArrayList uses a resizable array, HashMap uses hash-based buckets, and TreeMap maintains keys in sorted order. Generic type parameters are primarily enforced by the compiler through type erasure.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA monitoring application can store alert events in a List, track unique service identifiers in a Set, and map service names to health status in a Map. Choosing the collection according to lookup, ordering, and uniqueness requirements affects performance and correctness.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nUse List<Deployment> for ordered deployment records, Set<String> for unique environment names, and Map<String, DeploymentStatus> for status lookup. Compare ArrayList and HashSet membership behavior, and test duplicate values, missing keys, iteration, and null handling.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a concrete example to show the sequence of internal operations and identify the state before and after each important operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nFrequent errors include modifying a collection while iterating, using mutable map keys, assuming HashMap iteration order, selecting a list for repeated membership checks, ignoring null handling, and using raw types that bypass generic type checking.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe the important data structures, runtime rules, object relationships, processing stages, and decision points. Distinguish compile-time checks from runtime behavior and explain why the distinction matters during debugging.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
