        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "REST API Development — Common Errors and Failure Modes",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Common Errors and Failure Modes\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Identify realistic failures and explain their underlying causes. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: For each failure, connect the visible symptom to the likely technical cause. Separate configuration mistakes, programming defects, dependency failures, environment differences, resource exhaustion, and permission problems.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nReproduce one controlled failure, capture its error message, correct the root cause, and verify that the original operation succeeds.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nFor each failure, connect the visible symptom to the likely technical cause. Separate configuration mistakes, programming defects, dependency failures, environment differences, resource exhaustion, and permission problems.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "REST API Development — Debugging and Diagnostics",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Debugging and Diagnostics\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Provide a systematic method for diagnosing failures. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Start with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nUse a hypothesis-driven investigation: collect evidence, run one targeted check at a time, record the result, and verify the fix with the original failing operation.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nStart with the exact symptom, timestamp, affected component, and recent change. Explain which logs, stack traces, runtime values, build reports, process details, or network observations can confirm or eliminate each hypothesis.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-11",
        "title": "REST API Development — Performance and Optimization",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Performance and Optimization\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain the performance characteristics and optimization opportunities. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Identify relevant cost factors such as CPU work, memory allocation, I/O, network calls, locking, collection lookup, startup overhead, or dependency initialization. Explain how to measure the bottleneck before changing implementation.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nEstablish a baseline, change one performance factor, repeat the same workload, and compare latency, throughput, resource consumption, and correctness.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nIdentify relevant cost factors such as CPU work, memory allocation, I/O, network calls, locking, collection lookup, startup overhead, or dependency initialization. Explain how to measure the bottleneck before changing implementation.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-12",
        "title": "REST API Development — Security Considerations",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Security Considerations\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Identify security boundaries and controls relevant to this subject. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain input validation, trust boundaries, access control, secret handling, dependency risk, least privilege, secure defaults, and sensitive-data exposure where applicable. Describe how insecure configuration or implementation can be exploited.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nReview the implementation for one realistic security weakness, apply an appropriate control, and verify that unauthorized or malformed operations are rejected.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain input validation, trust boundaries, access control, secret handling, dependency risk, least privilege, secure defaults, and sensitive-data exposure where applicable. Describe how insecure configuration or implementation can be exploited.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-13",
        "title": "REST API Development — Production Integration",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Production Integration\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Explain how this subject integrates with a real production environment. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Describe its interactions with source control, CI pipelines, build artifacts, containers, runtime configuration, external services, monitoring, and deployment systems as relevant. Identify the contract between the application and each external component.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nTrace the feature from a source change through CI validation to deployment and runtime verification. Identify the evidence required at each boundary.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDescribe its interactions with source control, CI pipelines, build artifacts, containers, runtime configuration, external services, monitoring, and deployment systems as relevant. Identify the contract between the application and each external component.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-14",
        "title": "REST API Development — Operational Best Practices",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Operational Best Practices\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Define practices that keep this subject reliable during ongoing operation. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Cover repeatability, version control, controlled configuration, observability, resource limits, failure isolation, documentation, ownership, and change management. Explain which practices prevent incidents and which reduce recovery time.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nCreate an operational checklist with measurable checks, responsible ownership, and clear success or failure criteria.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nCover repeatability, version control, controlled configuration, observability, resource limits, failure isolation, documentation, ownership, and change management. Explain which practices prevent incidents and which reduce recovery time.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-15",
        "title": "REST API Development — Real-World Implementation",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Real-World Implementation\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Apply the subject to a realistic DevOps or application engineering requirement. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Translate a practical requirement into implementation decisions. Explain the selected design, the data flow, dependencies, failure handling, and how the result fits into a larger application delivery process.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nImplement a representative workflow and demonstrate its expected behavior with realistic input, output, and failure conditions.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nTranslate a practical requirement into implementation decisions. Explain the selected design, the data flow, dependencies, failure handling, and how the result fits into a larger application delivery process.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-16",
        "title": "REST API Development — Hands-on Validation",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Hands-on Validation\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Validate the implementation through observable and repeatable checks. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Define prerequisites, input data, execution steps, expected output, and cleanup. Explain how to distinguish a successful result from a command that merely exited without displaying an error.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nRun the validation steps in order, capture the actual result, and compare it against explicit acceptance criteria.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDefine prerequisites, input data, execution steps, expected output, and cleanup. Explain how to distinguish a successful result from a command that merely exited without displaying an error.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-17",
        "title": "REST API Development — Advanced Design Considerations",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Advanced Design Considerations\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Evaluate advanced design choices and their long-term consequences. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Discuss scalability, extensibility, concurrency, compatibility, failure isolation, lifecycle ownership, and trade-offs. Explain when a more complex design is justified and when a simpler design is safer.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nCompare at least two design approaches against reliability, maintainability, performance, and operational complexity.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nDiscuss scalability, extensibility, concurrency, compatibility, failure isolation, lifecycle ownership, and trade-offs. Explain when a more complex design is justified and when a simpler design is safer.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-12-section-18",
        "title": "REST API Development — Production Troubleshooting",
        "content": "# REST API Development — Production Troubleshooting\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nREST API development exposes application resources through HTTP methods, resource-oriented URLs, representations, status codes, and predictable request-response contracts.\n\nThis section focuses on Investigate a production-style incident involving this subject. The explanation is specific to **REST API Development** and should be understood in the context of its actual implementation, runtime behavior, and use in a Java application delivery workflow.\n\n## 2. Technical Foundation\n\nA Spring MVC request passes through servlet infrastructure, handler mappings, controller methods, argument resolution, validation, message conversion, and exception handling. HTTP semantics define safe methods, idempotency expectations, caching, and response status behavior.\n\nFor this section, pay particular attention to the following: Explain the impact assessment, initial containment, evidence preservation, diagnostic sequence, root-cause identification, safe recovery, and post-incident prevention. Include the difference between restoring service and permanently fixing the cause.\n\n## 3. Practical Engineering Example\n\nA deployment API may expose GET /deployments/{id} to retrieve a deployment, POST /deployments to create one, and DELETE /deployments/{id} to request removal. Validation and authorization must be applied before state-changing operations.\n\nA practical implementation approach for this topic is:\n\nImplement resource-oriented endpoints with appropriate HTTP methods, request validation, response DTOs, status codes, and centralized exception handling. Verify successful requests, invalid payloads, missing resources, and unauthorized requests using curl or an API test client.\n\nThe example should be adapted to the actual application requirement. Keep production configuration separate from local development settings, validate inputs at system boundaries, and make important runtime behavior observable.\n\n## 4. Implementation Procedure\n\nWork through a realistic incident timeline and specify the evidence needed before each potentially disruptive recovery action.\n\nWhen implementing this area, identify the required inputs, expected outputs, dependencies, and failure conditions before changing code. Keep each responsibility clear and make the implementation reproducible from a clean repository checkout.\n\n## 5. Common Problems and Root Causes\n\nCommon failures include returning incorrect status codes, accepting unvalidated input, exposing internal entity structures, ignoring pagination, failing to secure endpoints, and implementing retries without considering idempotency.\n\nAdditional problems to investigate include incorrect assumptions about defaults, incompatible dependency versions, missing configuration, invalid input, inadequate test coverage, and differences between local and production environments.\n\nDo not treat an error message as the root cause automatically. Identify the component that produced the error, inspect the operation that failed, and collect evidence before applying a change.\n\n## 6. Verification and Evidence\n\nVerify the implementation using the checks appropriate to this section:\n\n- Confirm that the required source files and configuration are present.\n- Run the relevant compiler, build, or validation command.\n- Execute a normal successful scenario.\n- Execute an invalid-input or failure scenario.\n- Inspect the actual output, logs, and exit status.\n- Confirm that the behavior remains correct after a clean build.\n\nA successful check must have an observable expected result. Record the command used, the important output, and the acceptance condition rather than relying on an assumption that the operation succeeded.\n\n## 7. DevOps Pipeline Integration\n\nIn a CI/CD workflow, this area should be validated at the earliest stage that has enough information to test it. Source-level checks belong near compilation and unit testing. Dependency, packaging, and integration checks belong before artifact promotion. Runtime and environment-dependent checks belong in deployment verification.\n\nKeep the artifact consistent between environments. Supply environment-specific values at deployment time, protect credentials, and retain enough build and deployment information to investigate a later incident.\n\n## 8. Production Engineering Considerations\n\nExplain the impact assessment, initial containment, evidence preservation, diagnostic sequence, root-cause identification, safe recovery, and post-incident prevention. Include the difference between restoring service and permanently fixing the cause.\n\nFor production use, define ownership, configuration control, logging expectations, failure behavior, and recovery steps. Changes should be reviewed and tested before release. Avoid manual production changes that cannot be reproduced or audited.\n\n## 9. Troubleshooting Approach\n\nWhen this area fails, first capture the exact error, the affected application version, the environment, and the time of failure. Determine whether the issue is caused by source code, runtime behavior, configuration, dependencies, permissions, or an external service.\n\nChange one relevant factor at a time. After applying a correction, repeat the original failing operation and run the related regression tests. A service appearing healthy after a restart is not sufficient proof that the underlying problem has been fixed.\n\n## 10. Engineering Outcome\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the relevant technical behavior, implement the required functionality, identify realistic failure modes, verify the result, and describe how the implementation is operated safely in a production DevOps environment."
      }
    ]
